1
00:00:01,850 --> 00:00:04,755
You blow the horn with everything you have.

2
00:00:04,805 --> 00:00:10,445
The sound rolls out over the moor, into the fog, toward the three lights.

3
00:00:10,745 --> 00:00:14,555
And for one long moment, nothing happens.

4
00:00:14,605 --> 00:00:20,525
Then, half a mile to the east, the next tower's beacon bursts into flame.

5
00:00:20,725 --> 00:00:27,615
By dawn, you'll know what those lights were. And one of the four missing men will still be alive.

6
00:00:27,665 --> 00:00:36,615
If you missed Part One: it's the year one hundred and thirty. You're Gaius, a soldier on Hadrian's Wall, at the very edge of the Roman Empire.

7
00:00:36,665 --> 00:00:41,235
Four scouts went north two days ago, and never came back.

8
00:00:41,285 --> 00:00:47,685
One of them is Felix. He sleeps in the bunk above yours, and he snores like a bear with a cold.

9
00:00:47,785 --> 00:00:52,840
And tonight, out on the moor, three lights were moving toward the Wall.

10
00:01:03,640 --> 00:01:08,440
The Romans didn't have radio. They had something almost as fast.

11
00:01:08,490 --> 00:01:15,210
Every tower on the Wall keeps a fire ready to light. One tower sees a signal, and lights its own.

12
00:01:15,260 --> 00:01:19,825
Then the next tower lights. And the next. And the next.

13
00:01:19,875 --> 00:01:26,615
In minutes, the warning has run for miles along the crags, a chain of fire in the dark.

14
00:01:26,665 --> 00:01:37,265
Historians still argue about exactly how the signals worked. Fire, smoke, flags, or trumpets. But the towers were built so that each one could see the next.

15
00:01:37,315 --> 00:01:43,515
Somewhere to the south, at the big fort, a trumpet answers. The cavalry are being woken.

16
00:01:43,565 --> 00:01:46,335
Below you, the milecastle wakes too.

17
00:01:46,385 --> 00:01:51,275
In the barracks, eight men go from sleep to armour in the time it takes to say it.

18
00:01:51,325 --> 00:01:56,285
Well. Seven men. Rufus sleeps through the horn, the trumpet, and the shouting.

19
00:01:56,335 --> 00:01:59,125
Somebody has to kick him. Twice.

20
00:01:59,175 --> 00:02:04,135
Helmets. Belts. Swords. Shields off the wall. Nobody talks.

21
00:02:04,185 --> 00:02:09,955
The centurion is already on the walkway, fully dressed. You wonder if he ever sleeps.

22
00:02:10,005 --> 00:02:17,665
He doesn't shout. He doesn't need to. He points. Shields there. Torches there. Every man to the parapet.

23
00:02:17,715 --> 00:02:24,055
The whole century is on the Wall now. Eighty men, shields up, staring into the fog.

24
00:02:24,105 --> 00:02:28,345
And out on the moor, the three lights have stopped moving.

25
00:02:28,395 --> 00:02:31,435
They just hang there. Waiting.

26
00:02:31,485 --> 00:02:35,000
You can feel eighty men holding their breath.

27
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:39,825
Then, one by one, the lights go out.

28
00:02:40,425 --> 00:02:46,900
And there's nothing. Just fog, and the dark, and the sound of your own heart.

29
00:02:46,950 --> 00:02:49,685
Now imagine what they saw.

30
00:02:49,735 --> 00:03:01,505
From out there in the heather, the Wall isn't sleeping any more. It's a line of fire, ten miles long. Torches. Shields. Eighty men on this stretch alone.

31
00:03:01,555 --> 00:03:08,150
Whoever was out there tonight wanted to know one thing. How fast does Rome wake up?

32
00:03:08,650 --> 00:03:10,450
Now they know.

33
00:03:15,385 --> 00:03:19,300
Nobody is allowed to leave the Wall. Not until it's light.

34
00:03:19,350 --> 00:03:22,555
Then, of course, it starts to rain.

35
00:03:22,605 --> 00:03:29,885
Cold, sideways, northern rain. It finds the gap between your helmet and your neck in under a minute.

36
00:03:29,935 --> 00:03:38,335
Water drips off the rim of your helmet. Your shield gets heavier as the leather soaks it up. Your feet went numb an hour ago.

37
00:03:38,385 --> 00:03:46,055
Someone whispers that at least the rain will put out the enemy's torches. Nobody laughs. Then everybody does.

38
00:03:46,105 --> 00:03:49,855
It's the only sound on the Wall for hours.

39
00:03:49,905 --> 00:03:55,765
You think about Felix. About the bunk above yours, empty for three nights now.

40
00:03:55,815 --> 00:04:02,270
You think about the helmet you found yesterday, full of bog water, laid on a rock like a message.

41
00:04:02,320 --> 00:04:06,180
And you wonder who the people out there really are.

42
00:04:06,230 --> 00:04:15,170
The Romans called them Caledonians, the people of the far north. In truth there were many tribes, with their own kings, and their own feuds.

43
00:04:15,220 --> 00:04:22,285
They didn't build walls. They didn't write anything down. Almost everything we know about them comes from their enemies.

44
00:04:22,335 --> 00:04:29,775
But we know they raided across this frontier again and again. And we know Rome never conquered them.

45
00:04:30,175 --> 00:04:37,535
Around you, the torches hiss in the rain. The sky in the east turns from black, to grey.

46
00:04:42,635 --> 00:04:47,755
At first light, you hear them before you see them. Hooves on the road.

47
00:04:47,805 --> 00:04:55,485
Thirty riders come up from the fort in the valley. Cavalry, in mail shirts, with long spears and oval shields.

48
00:04:55,535 --> 00:05:02,975
The horses steam in the cold air. The men on them look like they've been riding all night. Some of them have.

49
00:05:03,025 --> 00:05:13,805
They're auxiliaries. Most of the men guarding this Wall were. Not Roman citizens, but recruits from all over the Empire. Gaul, Germany, Spain, even Syria.

50
00:05:13,855 --> 00:05:20,035
Serve twenty-five years, and you get citizenship, for yourself and your children. That was the deal.

51
00:05:20,085 --> 00:05:27,985
The north gate creaks open. The riders go through, and you follow on foot, with five men from your century.

52
00:05:28,035 --> 00:05:32,225
Your centurion wants Roman eyes on whatever was out there.

53
00:05:37,325 --> 00:05:39,405
It isn't hard to find.

54
00:05:39,455 --> 00:05:46,525
Half a mile out, the heather has been trampled flat in a huge ring, a hundred feet across.

55
00:05:46,575 --> 00:05:53,365
Footprints. Hundreds of them. Bare feet, and leather shoes, pressed into the wet peat.

56
00:05:53,665 --> 00:06:00,235
And standing in the middle, three torches stuck in the ground. Burnt out. Still smoking.

57
00:06:00,285 --> 00:06:10,235
One of your men crouches and picks something out of the mud. A bronze brooch, the kind the tribes use to pin their cloaks. Somebody stood here long enough to lose it.

58
00:06:10,285 --> 00:06:18,285
So that's what the lights were. Not three men. Three torches, held up high, so the Wall would see them.

59
00:06:18,335 --> 00:06:24,455
Fifty men stood here last night, in the dark, waiting to see what Rome would do.

60
00:06:24,505 --> 00:06:29,105
Their tracks lead north. Not toward the Wall. Away from it.

61
00:06:29,605 --> 00:06:31,745
The horn turned them back.

62
00:06:31,795 --> 00:06:40,495
The cavalry commander looks at the tracks, then at you. He asks who blew the horn. You say you did. He nods. That's all.

63
00:06:40,545 --> 00:06:43,890
From a cavalry officer, that's practically a medal.

64
00:06:43,940 --> 00:06:50,265
The riders follow the tracks north. You and your five men search the moor to the west, toward the hills.

65
00:06:55,365 --> 00:07:01,905
Two miles out, there's a shepherd's hut. Stone walls, a turf roof, half sunk into the hillside.

66
00:07:01,955 --> 00:07:04,995
The door is shut. From the inside.

67
00:07:05,395 --> 00:07:08,742
You draw your sword. You knock with the hilt.

68
00:07:09,742 --> 00:07:13,722
And a voice says, in Latin: took you long enough.

69
00:07:14,322 --> 00:07:15,842
It's Felix.

70
00:07:15,892 --> 00:07:22,122
He's thin, and filthy, and frozen, wrapped in a stolen sheepskin. But he's alive.

71
00:07:22,172 --> 00:07:25,382
The first thing he asks is whether you brought any beer.

72
00:07:25,432 --> 00:07:29,572
You give him water instead. He drinks it like it's wine.

73
00:07:29,622 --> 00:07:31,737
Then he tells you what happened.

74
00:07:31,787 --> 00:07:38,347
Three nights ago, the scouts were caught in the open by a war band. Forty, maybe fifty men.

75
00:07:38,397 --> 00:07:42,097
They didn't kill them. They took them. Alive.

76
00:07:42,147 --> 00:07:50,987
Felix got free on the second night, and ran in the dark until he found this hut. He's been hiding here ever since, listening to them hunt for him.

77
00:07:51,037 --> 00:07:56,497
Twice, they came within a stone's throw of the door. Twice, they walked on.

78
00:07:56,547 --> 00:08:01,427
And he heard them talking. He knows enough of their language to understand.

79
00:08:01,477 --> 00:08:10,112
The war band was waiting for a signal. If the Wall was slow to wake, if the towers stayed dark, they would come over in the night.

80
00:08:10,162 --> 00:08:14,417
The helmet on the rock was a test. The lights were a test.

81
00:08:14,917 --> 00:08:17,387
And the horn was the answer.

82
00:08:17,987 --> 00:08:23,047
You ask about the other three. Felix doesn't answer. He just looks north.

83
00:08:28,147 --> 00:08:31,872
You carry Felix back to the Wall between two shields.

84
00:08:31,922 --> 00:08:37,392
When you reach the gate, the whole century is waiting. Even Rufus is awake.

85
00:08:37,442 --> 00:08:46,202
The centurion writes it all down. Three men taken. One escaped. A war band of fifty, turned back by the signal.

86
00:08:46,252 --> 00:08:52,722
By mid-morning, the report is in a dispatch rider's bag, galloping south down the military road.

87
00:08:52,772 --> 00:08:56,872
Within days, the governor of Britain will read your name in it.

88
00:08:56,922 --> 00:09:09,742
Reports like this really existed. At the fort of Vindolanda, archaeologists found hundreds of them, written on thin wooden tablets. Strength reports. Lists of the sick. Requests for more men.

89
00:09:09,792 --> 00:09:18,992
One of them is a note on how the Britons fight. No armour, it says. Very many cavalry. And it calls them the wretched little Britons.

90
00:09:19,042 --> 00:09:23,272
And at least one request for more beer. Felix would approve.

91
00:09:28,372 --> 00:09:33,672
The next morning, the sun comes up over the moor for the first time in a week.

92
00:09:33,722 --> 00:09:40,302
You're back on the walkway. Same spot. Same view. The brazier beside you is cold.

93
00:09:40,352 --> 00:09:46,592
Out there, the heather is gold, and the fog is lifting, and it all looks so peaceful.

94
00:09:46,642 --> 00:09:50,942
Somewhere beyond those hills are three men from your century.

95
00:09:50,992 --> 00:09:59,962
The Wall did its job last night. It didn't stop an army. It was never meant to. It was meant to see them coming, and wake everyone up.

96
00:10:00,362 --> 00:10:02,767
But it won't always be enough.

97
00:10:02,817 --> 00:10:13,622
About fifty years from now, a Roman historian named Cassius Dio will write that the tribes crossed the wall, and cut down a Roman general together with his soldiers.

98
00:10:13,672 --> 00:10:15,962
You won't live to see it.

99
00:10:16,562 --> 00:10:19,197
Today, the Wall held.

100
00:10:19,997 --> 00:10:23,472
Below you, in the barracks, someone is snoring again.

101
00:10:23,522 --> 00:10:26,642
It's the best sound you've ever heard.

102
00:10:27,142 --> 00:10:33,952
Next time: three men are still out there. And Rome doesn't leave its own behind. Or does it?
